                <pb facs="0885" n="878" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg878"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002/443: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-11-23T10:00:00+08:00">November 23, 1937—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 23—7 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1003. I learn confidentially that as no approach had been made to the customs
                    from the Japanese side and the Japanese Navy were continuing <pb facs="0886"
                        n="879" xml:id="pg879"/> busily to seize customs vessels the Shanghai
                    Commissioner of Customs yesterday went to see the Japanese Consul General who
                    told him that the Japanese being now in occupation of Shanghai require
                    “supervision” of the customs and asked the Commissioner to prepare proposals to
                    that end, saying that no “third parties” are to be concerned in the
                    discussions.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
